turns.content:
The facts support feeling blindsided, but they do not yet establish betrayal. “Betrayal” implies that Brenda knowingly violated a duty or commitment to you.

What matters is:

- who was responsible for submitting the financing objection;
- when Brenda received the information needed to submit it;
- whether she warned you before Monday at 5 p.m.;
- what the contract says about deadlines, notice, and release of the $12,000;
- whether you authorized the release or the contract permitted it automatically.

Immediately obtain the signed contract, financing-objection notice, delivery timestamps, deposit-release document, and all messages with Brenda and the lender. Ask Brenda in writing for a dated timeline and the contractual basis for releasing the deposit. Because $12,000 is at stake and deadlines may apply, have a local real-estate attorney review those records promptly.

Bottom line: blindsided accurately describes your reported experience; intentional or disloyal conduct is not established from the facts provided.
